  "answer": "The Aral Sea shrank primarily due to extensive irrigation projects in the 1960s that diverted water from the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers into the surrounding agricultural regions of Uzbekistan and Kazakhstan. This massive water diversion for cotton and other crops caused the sea to shrink significantly, with the South Aral Sea splitting into eastern and western basins by 2003. The Aral Sea was an endorheic salt lake lying between Kazakhstan to its north and Uzbekistan to its south, which began shrinking in the 1960s and had largely disappeared by the early 2000s."
